    "description": "The logistic growth curve plots population size against time as an S-shaped (sigmoidal) trace that starts with near-exponential increase, then bends over and levels off at the carrying capacity K. It captures the transition from resource-unlimited growth to resource limitation. It is the foundational model for density-dependent population regulation.",
    "dataNeeded": [
      {
        "name": "Time",
        "unit": "days"
      },
      {
        "name": "Population size",
        "unit": "count"
      },
      {
        "name": "Carrying capacity K",
        "unit": "count"
      }
    ],
    "whatItTellsYou": "The plateau height reveals the carrying capacity the environment can sustain; the steepness of the mid-curve reflects the intrinsic growth rate r. Data that overshoot and oscillate around K hint at time lags, while a curve still rising means the population has not yet hit its limit.",
